\title[What a Null $R^2$ Certifies]{What a Null $R^2$ Certifies:\\
Margin Ceilings, Contrast Inequalities, and Sign-Blindness\\
in a Quadratic-Character Effectivity Sweep}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{29 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A family of arguments in analytic number theory rests on an \emph{averaging assumption}: that when a quantity is averaged over many moduli, the argument may be blind to which factor it is examining at no cost. The assumption is used axiomatically. A weaker but valuable target is an \emph{effectivity criterion} --- a computable per-modulus test certifying that the assumption is realized. The natural candidate feature is the quadratic-character $L$-mass $P(m)=\sum_\chi |L(1,\chi)|$ over the nontrivial real Dirichlet characters modulo $m$.

We report a pre-registered sweep testing whether $P$ predicts the arithmetic-progression deviation field $D(m)=\max_a |\pi(x;m,a)-E|/\sqrt{E}$, $E=\pi(x)/\varphi(m)$, with the rule: $\Rsq>0.8$ arms the criterion, $\Rsq<0.5$ is an honest negative. The outcome is a null at two scales: $\Rsq=0.0187$ at $x=2^{26}$ ($287$ moduli, $\pi(x)=3{,}957{,}809$), with a \emph{negative} slope $-0.0767$, $\mathrm{CI}_{95}=(-0.136,-0.015)$; and $\Rsq=0.0785$ at $x=2^{28}$ ($2489$ moduli, $\pi(x)=14{,}630{,}843$). A cell-level secondary readout over $1902$ discriminant cells gives $\Rsq=0.00052$ with theory-signed slope $-0.034$, $\mathrm{CI}_{95}=[-0.101,+0.033]$. A pure size baseline against $\log m$ explains $\Rsq=0.790$.

The mathematical contribution is the deductive layer converting these numbers into theorems: a margin ceiling capping every threshold criterion at $\sqrt{\rho}$ sample standard deviations on a balanced split; an exact analysis-of-variance decomposition yielding pairwise, contrast, and group ceilings that are \emph{sharp at every cell count}; a size-domination bound showing a high baseline $\Rsq$ requires no arithmetic content; a proof that the registered permutation control has power exactly zero, together with an additive replacement whose two-point $p$-value is exactly $1/2$; and a proof that the sweep is sign-blind, so that every prescribed sign pattern of maximal character alignments is compatible with the recorded data. The averaging assumption remains axiomatic and effectivity remains open, but one route is now bounded with an explicit constant and one is proved untouched.
\end{abstract}

