\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The gap being attacked}
Let $\pi(x;m,a)$ count the primes $p\le x$ with $p\equiv a \pmod m$. For $\gcd(a,m)=1$ the equidistribution expectation is $E=\pi(x)/\varphi(m)$. Several barrier-style arguments about the control of these deviations depend on an averaging assumption whose informal content is \emph{which-factor blindness as identity}: over an average of moduli one may treat the moduli as interchangeable at no cost. The assumption is used as an axiom; its residual bookkeeping item, attacked here, is \textbf{effectivity}: is there a \emph{computable per-modulus criterion} deciding when the assumption is realized?

\subsection{Why quadratic-character $L$-values}
Real Dirichlet characters modulo $m$ carry the exceptional-zero phenomenon; $L(1,\chi)$ small is the analytic signature of an exceptional character; and classical error terms for primes in progressions are written in these quantities. The natural aggregate feature is
\[
P(m)=\sum_{\substack{\chi \bmod m\\ \chi \text{ real},\ \chi\ne\chi_0}} |L(1,\chi)|.
\]

\subsection{The pre-registration}
Fixed before any data were examined:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Primary response} $\displaystyle D(m)=\max_{a\in(\mathbb{Z}/m)^\times}\frac{|\pi(x;m,a)-E|}{\sqrt{E}}$;
\item \emph{secondary response} $\displaystyle \chi^2(m)=\sum_a \frac{(\pi(x;m,a)-E)^2}{E}$;
\item \emph{predictor} $P(m)$; \emph{model} least squares of $\log D$ on $\log P$;
\item \emph{verdict rule} $\Rsq>0.8 \Rightarrow$ criterion armed; $\Rsq<0.5 \Rightarrow$ honest negative.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The reporting problem}
Both stages returned the null. But $\Rsq=0.0785$ is a statistic, not a theorem: read literally it forbids nothing, and does not exclude a cleverer nonlinear criterion, an unusual threshold, or a many-celled partition. Sections \ref{sec:regression}--\ref{sec:cellgap} supply the deductive layer that removes the ambiguity. Sections \ref{sec:control}--\ref{sec:signblind} prove the two methodological items --- a provably vacuous control and provable sign-blindness --- that delimit the scope of the negative.

\section{The experiment and its numbers}

\subsection{Stage A: registered scale $x=2^{26}$}
$\pi(x)=3{,}957{,}809$; moduli squarefree $m\in[3,300]$ together with primes in $[307,997]$, $287$ in total; no shrinkage; wall time $9.3$\,s.
\begin{itemize}
\item Primary log--log fit: slope $-0.0767$, $\mathrm{CI}_{95}=(-0.136,-0.015)$, $\Rsq=0.0187$, bootstrap $[0.0007,0.065]$. The slope is \emph{negative}, opposite the effectivity narrative.
\item Partial $\Rsq$ controlling $\log\varphi(m)$: $0.0008$ --- the residual association is purely a size effect.
\item Secondary ($\chi^2$) readout agrees: $\Rsq=0.025$.
\item Control: cross-modulus pairing permutation, $2000$ draws, collapses to null (mean $0.0033$, max $0.0435$).
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Disclosed specification deviation.} The literal registered control --- a within-modulus count permutation --- is \emph{vacuous} for both readouts, because both are permutation invariant. This is a theorem, not a judgement call; see Section~\ref{sec:control}. A cross-modulus pairing control was substituted.

\subsection{Stage B: scaled artifact $x=2^{28}$}
$\pi(x)=14{,}630{,}843$; $2489$ moduli (dense $2\ldots 1500$ plus primes beyond); wall time $247.7$\,s.
\begin{itemize}
\item Primary per-modulus carrier: $\Rsq=0.0785$ --- null.
\item Cell-level secondary $y\sim\log(1/L)$ over $1902$ discriminant cells: $\Rsq=0.00052$; theory-signed slope $B=-0.034$, $\mathrm{CI}_{95}=[-0.101,+0.033]$.
\item \emph{Size baseline}: $D$ on $\log m$ alone gives $\Rsq=0.790$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Verification of the $L$-value path}
For the fundamental discriminant $-3$ one has $h(-3)=1$, $w=6$, and the class-number formula gives exactly
\[
L(1,\chi_{-3}) \;=\; \frac{2\pi h(-3)}{w\sqrt{3}} \;=\; \frac{\pi}{3\sqrt{3}} \;\approx\; 0.604600 .
\]
The truncated series was calibrated on $226$ overlap discriminants with median relative error $1.8\times10^{-5}$; the final run's truncation quality had median real-share $8.7\times10^{-4}$ and worst relative error $8.2\times10^{-2}$.

\begin{remark}[Ledger]
An off-by-one in the character table corrupted non-exact $L$-values (the character mod $5$ returned $0.127$ against the true $0.430$); it was caught by a spot check against exact values, fixed, and the run repeated before any fit was recorded. A smoke control gate failed at $n=29$, resolving at the scale where the null collapses cleanly. A Mertens gate failed by its own strict pre-stated rule (slope $0.9277$, $\mathrm{CI}_{95}=[0.9234,0.9320]$, $\Rsq=0.9894$: near-proportional but just outside the band), with implied $\bar{K}=-0.216$ inside the theoretical bound $|K|\le 0.756$.
\end{remark}

\section{Finite-sample regression, exactly}\label{sec:regression}

All statements below are exact algebra on a finite sample: no asymptotics, no distributional assumptions, no model-correctness hypothesis.

\begin{definition}
Let $\iota$ be a finite nonempty index set of size $n$ and $y:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$ the response. Write $\bar{y}=n^{-1}\sum_i y_i$, $\|u\|^2=\sum_i u_i^2$, and $\TSS(y)=\|y-\bar{y}\|^2$. For a nonempty class $T$ of predictors,
\[
\RSS(y,T)=\inf_{h\in T}\|y-h\|^2, \qquad \Rsq(y,T)=1-\frac{\RSS(y,T)}{\TSS(y)} .
\]
The \emph{affine class} of a feature $x$ is $\Aclass(x)=\{i\mapsto \alpha+\beta x_i\}$; the \emph{measurable class} of a feature $P$ is $\Mclass(P)=\{g\circ P : g \text{ arbitrary}\}$, comprising \emph{every} function of $P$. Note $\Aclass(x)\subseteq\Mclass(x)$.
\end{definition}

A criterion is a function of the feature; bounding $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))$ therefore bounds every criterion simultaneously.

\begin{definition}
For a feature $P:\iota\to\alpha$, the \emph{cell} at an attained level $c$ is $\cell(P,c)=\{i: P_i=c\}$ of size $n_c$, with \emph{cell mean} $m_c=n_c^{-1}\sum_{i\in\cell(P,c)}y_i$. Set
\[
\withinSS(y,P)=\sum_c \sum_{i\in\cell(P,c)}(y_i-m_c)^2, \qquad
\betweenSS(y,P)=\sum_c n_c\,(m_c-\bar{y})^2 .
\]
\end{definition}

\section{What a null $\Rsq$ certifies}

\begin{lemma}[Exact two-group decomposition]\label{lem:twogroup}
Let $S\subseteq\iota$ with $S,S^{c}$ both nonempty, and let $\hat{g}$ be the two-valued predictor taking the group means on $S$ and on $S^{c}$. Then
\[
\|y-\hat{g}\|^2 \;=\; \TSS(y) - \frac{n_1 n_2}{n}\,(m_1-m_2)^2,
\]
with $n_1=|S|$, $n_2=|S^{c}|$ and $m_1,m_2$ the group means.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split $\sum_i (y_i-\bar{y})^2$ over the two groups; each contributes its within-group energy plus $n_j(m_j-\bar{y})^2$. Substituting $\bar{y}=(n_1m_1+n_2m_2)/n$ collapses the two offset terms into the single pooled term $(n_1n_2/n)(m_1-m_2)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A criterion must show up in $\Rsq$]\label{thm:margin}
Let $P$ be a feature and $t$ a threshold whose induced split separates the response two-sidedly with margin $\delta\ge 0$:
\[
P_i\ge t \implies y_i \ge \mu+\delta, \qquad P_i<t \implies y_i \le \mu-\delta .
\]
If both groups are nonempty, with sizes $n_1,n_2$, then
\[
\frac{4\delta^2 n_1 n_2}{n} \;\le\; \Rsq\big(y,\Mclass(P)\big)\cdot \TSS(y).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two-valued predictor of Lemma~\ref{lem:twogroup} depends on $i$ only through the truth of $P_i\ge t$, hence lies in $\Mclass(P)$; so $\RSS(y,\Mclass(P))\le\|y-\hat{g}\|^2$ and, by Lemma~\ref{lem:twogroup}, $\Rsq\cdot\TSS \ge (n_1n_2/n)(m_1-m_2)^2$. The separation hypotheses give $m_1\ge\mu+\delta$, $m_2\le\mu-\delta$, whence $(m_1-m_2)^2\ge 4\delta^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Margin ceiling]\label{cor:ceiling}
If $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))\le\rho$, then every threshold criterion on $P$ obeys $4\delta^2 n_1n_2/n \le \rho\,\TSS(y)$. For a balanced split $n_1=n_2=n/2$ this is $\delta^2 n \le \rho\,\TSS(y)$, i.e.\ $\delta\le\sqrt{\rho}\,s$ with $s$ the sample standard deviation.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Recorded instance]
With the stage-B ceiling $\rho=0.0785$, every threshold criterion built from the quadratic-character $L$-mass --- of any functional form --- separates the deviation field with margin at most $\delta\le\sqrt{0.0785}\,s\approx 0.280\,s$ on a balanced split.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Incremental ceiling over a baseline]
Let $g$ be a baseline predictor, $v$ a feature, and $T$ a class containing $g+tv$ for all $t\in\mathbb{R}$. If $\Rsq(y,T)\le \Rsq(y,g)+\Delta$, then $\ip{y-g}{v}^2 \le \Delta\,\|v\|^2\,\TSS(y)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Optimizing over the one-parameter family gives $\Rsq(y,T)\ge \Rsq(y,g)+\ip{y-g}{v}^2/(\|v\|^2\TSS)$; rearrange.
\end{proof}

This is the form in which the stage-A partial $\Rsq$ of $0.0008$ constrains the criterion: a genuine criterion would have appeared as a residual correlation after the size baseline.

\begin{proposition}[Nonlinear residual floor]
If $\theta\,\TSS(y)\le\withinSS(y,P)$ then $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))\le 1-\theta$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The minimizer in $\Mclass(P)$ is the cell-mean function, with residual energy exactly $\withinSS(y,P)$.
\end{proof}

This is the form in which the cell-level readout ($\Rsq=0.00052$ over $1902$ discriminant cells) constrains the criterion.

\section{Beyond thresholds: the cell-gap calculus}\label{sec:cellgap}

\begin{theorem}[Analysis of variance]\label{thm:anova}
For any response $y$ and any feature $P$,
\[
\TSS(y) \;=\; \withinSS(y,P) + \betweenSS(y,P).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition the index set into the fibers of $P$ and apply cell by cell the identity $\sum_{i\in C}(y_i-\bar{y})^2=\sum_{i\in C}(y_i-m_C)^2+|C|(m_C-\bar{y})^2$, valid for $|C|\ne 0$; sum over cells.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Explained energy of the whole nonlinear class]\label{cor:between}
If $\TSS(y)>0$ then $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))\cdot\TSS(y)=\betweenSS(y,P)$.
\end{corollary}

Thus a recorded ceiling $\rho$ is exactly a budget on between-cell energy: $\betweenSS(y,P)\le\rho\,\TSS(y)$.

\begin{lemma}[Two-cell energy inequality]\label{lem:twocell}
For $n_a,n_b>0$ and any reference level $m$,
\[
\frac{n_a n_b}{n_a+n_b}\,(m_a-m_b)^2 \;\le\; n_a(m_a-m)^2 + n_b(m_b-m)^2 ,
\]
with equality exactly at the pooled mean $m=(n_am_a+n_bm_b)/(n_a+n_b)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Clearing denominators, the difference equals $\big(n_a(m_a-m)-n_b(m-m_b)\big)^2/(n_a+n_b)\ge 0$, vanishing precisely at the pooled mean.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cell-gap ceiling]\label{thm:cellgap}
Suppose $\TSS(y)>0$ and $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))\le\rho$. Then for any two distinct attained levels $a\ne b$,
\[
(m_a-m_b)^2 \;\le\; \rho\,\TSS(y)\left(\frac{1}{n_a}+\frac{1}{n_b}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two cells' contributions form a sub-sum of the nonnegative sum $\betweenSS$, hence are at most $\rho\,\TSS$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:between}. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:twocell} with $m=\bar{y}$ and divide by the pooled coefficient.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multi-cell contrast ceiling]\label{thm:contrast}
Let $S$ be any finite set of attained levels of $P$ and $w:S\to\mathbb{R}$ any weights. If $\TSS(y)>0$ and $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))\le\rho$, then
\[
\Big(\sum_{c\in S} w_c\,(m_c-\bar{y})\Big)^2 \;\le\; \rho\,\TSS(y)\sum_{c\in S}\frac{w_c^2}{n_c}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $w_c(m_c-\bar{y})=(w_c/\sqrt{n_c})\cdot(\sqrt{n_c}(m_c-\bar{y}))$ and apply Cauchy--Schwarz; the second factor squares to a sub-sum of $\betweenSS$, bounded via Corollary~\ref{cor:between}.
\end{proof}

When $\sum_c w_c=0$ the left-hand side is a genuine contrast, invariant under shifting $y$ by a constant. Taking $w=(+1,-1)$ on a pair recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:cellgap}.

\begin{definition}
For a set $A$ of attained levels put $N_A=\sum_{c\in A} n_c$ and $M_A=N_A^{-1}\sum_{c\in A} n_c m_c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Group-gap ceiling: the form a criterion takes]\label{thm:group}
Let $A,B$ be disjoint sets of attained levels with $N_A,N_B>0$. If $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))\le\rho$, then
\[
(M_A-M_B)^2 \;\le\; \rho\,\TSS(y)\left(\frac{1}{N_A}+\frac{1}{N_B}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:contrast} with $w_c=n_c/N_A$ on $A$ and $w_c=-n_c/N_B$ on $B$. The weights sum to zero, the left side becomes $(M_A-M_B)^2$, and the weight budget telescopes to $1/N_A+1/N_B$.
\end{proof}

Any decision rule reading the $L$-mass sorts the moduli into a union of cells $A$ and a disjoint union $B$; with $\rho=0.0785$ this is the operative certificate of the paper.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness at every cell count]\label{thm:sharp}
Suppose $y_i=g(P_i)$ for some $g$. Then $\withinSS(y,P)=0$, hence $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))=1$; the weights $w_c=n_c(m_c-\bar{y})$ satisfy $\sum_c w_c=0$ and turn Theorem~\ref{thm:contrast} into an exact equality with $\rho=1$, for an arbitrary number of cells:
\[
\Big(\sum_c w_c(m_c-\bar{y})\Big)^2 \;=\; 1\cdot\TSS(y)\cdot\sum_c \frac{w_c^2}{n_c}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $y$ is a function of $P$ it is constant on cells, so $\withinSS=0$ and $\TSS=\betweenSS=:\mathcal{E}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:anova}. With the stated weights both sides equal $\mathcal{E}^2$. The contrast property follows from $\sum_c n_c m_c=n\bar{y}$ and $\sum_c n_c=n$.
\end{proof}

For a two-valued feature this specializes to $(m_a-m_b)^2=\TSS(1/n_a+1/n_b)$, exact equality in Theorem~\ref{thm:cellgap}. Hence the pairwise constant was already optimal: the conjectured extra hiding room for a multi-cell criterion inside a null $\Rsq$ does not exist.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sqnorm}
For all $u,v$: $\ \|u+v\|^2 \ge \tfrac{1}{2}\|u\|^2 - \|v\|^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pointwise $(u_i+v_i)^2-\tfrac12 u_i^2+v_i^2=\tfrac12(u_i+2v_i)^2\ge 0$; sum. No Cauchy--Schwarz is needed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Size domination is free]\label{thm:size}
Suppose $y_i-\bar{y}=b(x_i-\bar{x})+r_i$ with $\|r\|^2\le\eta$, and $2\eta < b^2\|\tilde{x}\|^2$ where $\tilde{x}=x-\bar{x}$. Then
\[
\Rsq\big(y,\Aclass(x)\big) \;\ge\; 1-\frac{\eta}{\,b^2\|\tilde{x}\|^2/2-\eta\,}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:sqnorm} with $u=b\tilde{x}$, $v=r$ gives $\TSS(y)\ge b^2\|\tilde{x}\|^2/2-\eta=:T_0>0$. The affine predictor with slope $b$ has residual exactly $r$, so $\RSS(y,\Aclass(x))\le\eta$, whence $\Rsq\ge 1-\eta/T_0$.
\end{proof}

Near-affinity in a single covariate is therefore \emph{sufficient} for a high $\Rsq$. Since the normalized deviations grow with $\varphi(m)$ for elementary reasons, the observed size baseline $\Rsq=0.790$ is exactly what Theorem~\ref{thm:size} predicts, and carries no arithmetic information.

\begin{theorem}[Effectivity dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Fix a sample with response $y$, criterion feature $P$, and size covariate $x$. Assume $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P))\le\rho$ and a near-affine size decomposition as in Theorem~\ref{thm:size}. Then simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every threshold criterion on $P$ with two-sided margin $\delta$ obeys $4\delta^2 n_1n_2/n \le \rho\,\TSS(y)$; and
\item as soon as $\rho < 1-\eta/(b^2\|\tilde{x}\|^2/2-\eta)$, the single size covariate strictly outperforms the entire, arbitrarily nonlinear class of functions of $P$: $\Rsq(y,\Mclass(P)) < \Rsq(y,\Aclass(x))$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

With $\rho=0.0785$ against a size baseline of $0.790$, both clauses fire on the recorded data.

\section{The registered control had power exactly zero --- and its repair}\label{sec:control}

Model a modulus's data as a count field $c:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$ over residue classes with expectation $E>0$, and set
\[
\maxDev(c,E)=\frac{\max_a |c_a-E|}{\sqrt{E}}, \qquad
\chi^2(c,E)=\frac{\sum_a (c_a-E)^2}{E}.
\]

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:perminv}
Both readouts are invariant under relabeling of the classes: $\maxDev(c\circ\sigma,E)=\maxDev(c,E)$ and $\chi^2(c\circ\sigma,E)=\chi^2(c,E)$ for every permutation $\sigma$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
The one-sided permutation $p$-value of a statistic $T$ at $c$ is the fraction of permutations $\sigma$ with $T(c)\le T(c\circ\sigma)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The permutation control cannot reject]\label{thm:vacuous}
For any relabeling-invariant statistic and any count field, the permutation $p$-value equals exactly $1$. In particular both registered readouts have $p\equiv 1$: the within-modulus permutation control has power exactly zero, whatever the arithmetic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Invariance makes $T(c)\le T(c\circ\sigma)$ true for every $\sigma$, so the filtered set is the whole permutation group.
\end{proof}

We now replace relabeling by an \emph{additive} perturbation $c\mapsto c+t\,w$ along a direction $w$ nonzero at some class $a_0$.

\begin{lemma}[Exact amplitude profile of $\chi^2$]\label{lem:profile}
With $A=\sum_a w_a^2$ and $B=\sum_a (c_a-E)w_a$,
\[
\chi^2(c+tw,E) \;=\; \chi^2(c,E) + \frac{2tB+t^2A}{E}\qquad\text{for all } t .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[One-sided rejection]
For any $w$ with $w_{a_0}\ne 0$ there is $t_0>0$ such that for all $t\ge t_0$ both $\maxDev(c+tw,E)>\maxDev(c,E)$ and $\chi^2(c+tw,E)>\chi^2(c,E)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $\chi^2$: $A>0$, so $2tB+t^2A>0$ once $t>2|B|/A$. For $\maxDev$: at $a_0$ the perturbed field is at distance at least $t|w_{a_0}|-|c_{a_0}-E|$ from $E$, exceeding the unperturbed maximum $M$ once $t|w_{a_0}|>2M+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetric identity, and rejection at every amplitude]\label{thm:sym}
\[
\chi^2(c+tw,E)+\chi^2(c-tw,E) \;=\; 2\chi^2(c,E)+\frac{2t^2A}{E},
\]
hence for every $t\ne 0$, $\ \chi^2(c,E) < \max\{\chi^2(c+tw,E),\ \chi^2(c-tw,E)\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Add the profile of Lemma~\ref{lem:profile} at $t$ and $-t$; the linear terms cancel and the excess $2t^2A/E$ is strictly positive, so the two perturbed values cannot both fall below the observed one.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convex amplitude profile for the primary readout]
The map $t\mapsto \maxDev(c+tw,E)$ is convex. Hence the acceptance region $\{t : \maxDev(c+tw,E)<\maxDev(c,E)\}$ is an interval, and for every $t$,
\[
\maxDev(c,E) \le \max\{\maxDev(c+tw,E),\ \maxDev(c-tw,E)\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $t\mapsto|c_a+tw_a-E|$ is convex, a finite maximum of convex functions is convex, and dividing by $\sqrt{E}>0$ preserves convexity. Sublevel sets of a convex function on $\mathbb{R}$ are intervals; the last display is convexity at the midpoint $0$ of $\{t,-t\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact two-point $p$-value]\label{thm:twopoint}
Define the two-point $p$-value of $T$ under $c\mapsto c\pm tw$ as the fraction of the two perturbations with $T(c)\le T(\cdot)$. If $B\ne0$, $t>0$ and $tA<2|B|$, then the two-point $p$-value of $\chi^2$ is exactly $1/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:profile} the increments at $\pm t$ are $(2tB+t^2A)/E$ and $(-2tB+t^2A)/E$; the hypothesis $tA<2|B|$ makes exactly one positive and the other negative.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
On the same data the relabeling $p$-value of the $\chi^2$ readout is exactly $1$ (power zero) while the additive two-point $p$-value is exactly $1/2$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Sign-blindness: the exact scope of the negative}\label{sec:signblind}

The registered readouts are magnitudes; the object the underlying theory cares about is the \emph{signed alignment} $\ip{c}{w}=\sum_a c_a w_a$ of the count field with a character weight.

\begin{lemma}[Reflection flips alignment]\label{lem:flip}
Let $\nu$ be a permutation of the classes under which $w$ is odd, $w_{\nu(a)}=-w_a$ for all $a$. Then $\ip{c\circ\nu}{w}=-\ip{c}{w}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}[General separation]
Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:flip}, if $\ip{c}{w}\ne 0$ then for every relabeling-invariant statistic $T$ one has $T(c\circ\nu)=T(c)$ while $\ip{c\circ\nu}{w}\ne\ip{c}{w}$.
\end{proposition}

Let $p$ be prime and $\chi$ the real-valued quadratic character mod $p$, so $\chi(a)=\pm1$ for $a\ne0$ and $\chi(0)=0$. Then $\sum_a\chi(a)=0$ for $p\ne2$, and for $p\equiv3\pmod 4$ the character is \emph{odd}: $\chi(-a)=-\chi(a)$.

\begin{lemma}[Maximal alignment of a tilted field]\label{lem:tilt}
For $p\ne2$ and any $E$, the field $c(a)=E+\chi(a)$ satisfies $\ip{c}{\chi}=\sum_a \chi(a)^2=p-1$, the maximum available.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The readouts cannot see alignment]\label{thm:blind}
For every prime $p\equiv3\pmod 4$ and every level $E$ there are count fields $c_1,c_2$ on the classes mod $p$ with
\[
\maxDev(c_1,E)=\maxDev(c_2,E), \qquad \chi^2(c_1,E)=\chi^2(c_2,E),
\]
while $\ip{c_1}{\chi}=p-1$ and $\ip{c_2}{\chi}=-(p-1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $c_1(a)=E+\chi(a)$ and $c_2=c_1\circ\nu$ with $\nu:a\mapsto -a$. Lemma~\ref{lem:perminv} gives equality of readouts, since reflection is a relabeling; Lemmas~\ref{lem:tilt} and \ref{lem:flip} give the alignments.
\end{proof}

Let the sample consist of one count field per modulus, $c:\iota\to(\kappa\to\mathbb{R})$, with expectations $E_i$, and define the sweep response $y_i=\maxDev(c_i,E_i)$.

\begin{theorem}[The sweep is blind to the sign of the alignment]
Reflect each modulus's field by a permutation $\sigma_i$ under which the character weight $w$ is odd. Then the response vector is unchanged, hence $\Rsq$ is unchanged in \emph{every} model class whatsoever, while $\ip{c_i\circ\sigma_i}{w}=-\ip{c_i}{w}$ for every $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Every sign pattern fits the same data]\label{thm:allsigns}
Fix a prime $p\equiv3\pmod4$ and any prescribed pattern of signs $s:\iota\to\{\pm\}$. There is a sample of count fields on the classes mod $p$ realizing that pattern of maximal alignments $\pm(p-1)$ whose recorded response vector --- and therefore whose fitted $\Rsq$ in every model class --- is identical to that of the unreflected sample.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $c_i$ to be the tilted field $E_i+\chi$ where $s_i=+$ and its reflection where $s_i=-$; apply the previous theorem for the response and Lemmas~\ref{lem:tilt}, \ref{lem:flip} for the alignments.
\end{proof}

Consequently the recorded magnitude null places \emph{no constraint whatsoever} on the signed character-alignment route. This is the paper's prominent scoping caveat, proved rather than asserted: a signed analysis is a strictly finer instrument and must be run before the $L$-value route is declared dead.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. Segmented residue-class prime census.} Sieve $[2,x]$ in cache-sized segments; for each modulus maintain a length-$m$ tally incremented at $p\bmod m$. Then $D(m)=\max_{\gcd(a,m)=1}|\mathrm{tally}_a-E|/\sqrt{E}$ with $E=\pi(x)/\varphi(m)$. Cost $O(x\log\log x)$ for the sieve plus $O(\pi(x)\cdot|\mathcal{M}|)$ for tallying; this dominates the wall time.

\textbf{B. Quadratic-character $L$-mass with exact calibration.} For each fundamental discriminant $d$, evaluate $L(1,\chi_d)$ by a truncated smoothed Dirichlet series, calibrated where the class-number formula applies via $L(1,\chi_d)=2\pi h(d)/(w\sqrt{|d|})$ for $d<0$. The anchor $L(1,\chi_{-3})=\pi/(3\sqrt{3})$ validated the path.

\textbf{C. Ceiling certification.} Bin the moduli by $P$, compute $n_c$ and $m_c$, verify $\TSS=\withinSS+\betweenSS$ numerically, then evaluate the certified bounds --- the balanced margin $\sqrt{\rho}\,s$, the pairwise gap $\sqrt{\rho\,\TSS(1/n_a+1/n_b)}$, and for any grouping the group ceiling $\sqrt{\rho\,\TSS(1/N_A+1/N_B)}$ --- against the observed gaps. Cost $O(n)$ after an $O(n\log n)$ sort.

\section{Applications and reusability}

Nothing in Sections \ref{sec:regression}--\ref{sec:control} is specific to number theory.
\begin{itemize}
\item Any pre-registered regression sweep reporting a small $\Rsq$ has implicitly proved a margin ceiling: Corollary~\ref{cor:ceiling} converts ``we found no signal'' into ``no threshold rule on this feature separates by more than $\sqrt{\rho}$ standard deviations''.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:group} bounds what any grouping rule reading a candidate feature can achieve --- a distribution-free guarantee, exact on the sample at hand.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:size} quantifies the fact that a size covariate can score a high $\Rsq$ for free; reporting a baseline without this bound over-credits it.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:vacuous} is a checklist item with teeth: if the test statistic is invariant under the randomization, the control has power exactly zero. Symmetric readouts paired with relabeling randomizations are a common silent failure mode, and the additive family is a drop-in repair with a computable $p$-value.
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:allsigns} is the general moral of sign-blindness: a magnitude-only readout can leave an entire signed hypothesis class formally unconstrained regardless of sample size.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

\textbf{What the null establishes.} The magnitude route to a computable effectivity criterion is capped at toy scale by an explicit constant: any function of $P(m)$, at any resolution, with any decision rule, obeys the ceilings above, and those ceilings are sharp (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}).

\textbf{What it does not.} It does not weaken the barrier program: the averaging assumption stays axiomatic at practical scale and effectivity stays an open gap item. And it cannot touch the signed route (Theorem~\ref{thm:allsigns}) --- the caveat that must travel with every citation.

\textbf{Why the slope came out negative.} At stage A the slope was $-0.0767$ and the partial $\Rsq$ after $\log\varphi(m)$ was $0.0008$: the residual association is a size effect. Larger moduli carry more characters, hence larger $L$-mass, and simultaneously smaller expected counts per class. What looks like a faint negative arithmetic trend is a size artifact, exactly as the $0.790$ baseline plus Theorem~\ref{thm:size} predict.

\textbf{Scale.} Both scales are toy. The ceilings are exact, unconditional finite-sample statements about \emph{this} sample; no extrapolation to $x\to\infty$ is claimed. Their value is that they are theorems about the sample rather than inferences about a population, which is what one wants when reporting a negative.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The signed route, run properly.} Regress the \emph{signed} alignments $\ip{c_m}{\chi}$ --- not their magnitudes --- against signed $L$-value data, with a randomization that is not invariant under reflection. Theorem~\ref{thm:allsigns} shows the present data cannot prejudge the outcome.
\item \textbf{Scale.} Push to $x=2^{32}$ and beyond and track whether the certified $\rho$ moves; a shrinking ceiling is a much stronger negative, a growing one reopens the magnitude route.
\item \textbf{Sharper size deconfounding.} Replace $\log m$ with a theory-derived normalization and certify the ceiling on the residual field, tightening $\rho$.
\item \textbf{Adaptive ceilings.} Extend the contrast calculus to data-selected features via a uniform bound over a family of candidates.
\item \textbf{Other candidate features.} The machinery is feature-agnostic; applying it to class numbers, discriminant factorization statistics, or smoothness features would map the effectivity landscape rather than probing a single point.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A pre-registered sweep asked whether quadratic-character $L$-value magnitude carries effectivity information for an averaging assumption governing primes in arithmetic progressions. By rules fixed in advance the answer at two scales is no: $\Rsq=0.0187$ at $x=2^{26}$ and $\Rsq=0.0785$ at $x=2^{28}$, against a size baseline of $0.790$. The contribution is what was made of that answer: the null was converted into a family of exact, sharp, finite-sample ceilings bounding every criterion the feature can support --- threshold, multi-cell, or grouped --- at $0.28$ sample standard deviations of separation; the registered control was proved to have power exactly zero and given a working replacement with $p$-value exactly $1/2$; and the scope of the negative was delimited precisely, with the signed character-alignment route formally untouched. A negative result is usually an absence. Made into theorems, it becomes a map.

